<section id="faq" class="py-16 lg:py-20" style="background-color: rgba(245, 239, 230, 0.4);">
  <div class="max-w-[900px] mx-auto px-4">
    <h2 class="text-[28px] lg:text-[36px] font-bold text-[#1A1A1A] font-serif mb-10 leading-[1.2]">
      Frequently Asked Questions About IRS and State Tax Audits
    </h2>

    <div class="space-y-8">
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-[20px] lg:text-[22px] font-bold text-[#1A1A1A] font-serif mb-3">
          How long does a typical audit take?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65]">
          Audit duration varies significantly based on complexity and type. Correspondence audits typically resolve within 3–6 months, while office audits average 6–12 months. Field audits can extend 12–18 months or longer.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="text-[20px] lg:text-[22px] font-bold text-[#1A1A1A] font-serif mb-3">
          Can penalties be reduced or eliminated?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65]">
          Many penalties can be abated through reasonable cause arguments. First-time penalty abatement, reasonable cause based on circumstances beyond your control, and statutory exceptions provide relief opportunities.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h3 class="text-[20px] lg:text-[22px] font-bold text-[#1A1A1A] font-serif mb-3">
          Should I handle an audit myself to save money?
        </h3>
        <p class="text-[#374151] text-[16px] leading-[1.65]">
          While possible, self-representation rarely proves cost-effective. Professional representation typically saves more in reduced assessments than the representation cost. More importantly, experienced attorneys prevent mistakes that could trigger expanded examinations or criminal referrals.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
